AB  - Abrolhos is the largest and most diverse coral reef system in the South Atlantic and hosts several endemic coral species. In addition to increasing sea surface temperatures associated with contemporary climate change, this ecosystem is threatened by local to regional scale human activities, including dredging, inland deforestation, and enhanced river basin erosion, which contribute to increased coastal sediment discharge. In November 2015, the collapse of the Fundao dam, one of the largest environmental disasters in the Southern Hemisphere, released large volumes of iron-rich tailings into the coastal ocean, raising concerns about its potential influence on sediment dynamics reaching the Abrolhos region. As part of a collaborative monitoring effort involving universities, research foundations, and governmental institutions, we present the first results of a monitoring program aimed at characterizing total suspended matter (TSM) in surface waters of the Abrolhos coastal system using Sentinel-3 OLCI data. We evaluated a commonly used Global satellite-based TSM retrieval algorithm and developed a regionally calibrated empirical algorithm based on extensive in situ observations. The regional algorithm showed comparable overall error magnitudes but reduced systematic bias relative to the global product when applied to this optically complex coastal environment. Analysis of approximately two years of satellite observations (2019-2020), based on daily imagery, revealed a pronounced seasonal variability in TSM concentrations, with higher values during winter and spring. TSM variability was associated with wind intensity, indicating that wind-driven sediment resuspension may represent an important influence on suspended sediment dynamics in the Abrolhos region. The proposed approach provides a robust framework for long-term monitoring of sediment variability and supports improved assessments of environmental pressures affecting coral reef ecosystems.
