TI  - PRESENT KNOWLEDGE OF SYSTEMATICS AND ZOOGEOGRAPHY OF ORDER GORGONACEA IN HAWAII
T2  - PACIFIC SCIENCE
LA  - English
AB  - Past knowledge of the order Gorgonacea in Hawaii is based almost exclusively on the collections of the USA Fish Commission steamer Albatross in 1902, which contain 52 spp. Recent efforts to investigate the ecology of precious coral produced a new collection based on 183 dredge hauls and 10 dives with a submersible. This program is collectively referred to as the Sango Expedition. Of 59 spp. of gorgonians obtained by the Sango Expedition, 13 are considered new [identified to generic level only] and 28 are new geographic records; the total number of species considered to be present in Hawaii is 93 spp. In constrast to the high diversity of gorgonians in the west Indies and the Indo-West-Pacific, the faunal list in Hawaii must still be considered depauperate. This is expecially true in shallow water (< 75 m), where only 1 sp. is known. Although climatic deterioration during the Pleistocene could account for the scarcity of gorgonians in shallow water at the present time, this factor is unlikely to have affected deeper species. A modern complement of an ancestral fauna in shallow water would be expected as in the case of reef corals. The paucity of gorgonians in Hawaii may be due to isolation, which appears to have been a particularly effective barrier in shallow water. The only accessible route to Hawaii for gorgonians has been in deep water where, in the past, there were numerous stepping stones that may have aided dispersal. Chemical and physical gradients in deep water are relatively low. Why more deepwater species have not migrated into shallow water in Hawaii may be a reflection of their stenotypic character.
