TI  - Oysters or maerl? The Navy's dilemma faced with the greed of the Brest Agricultural Society (1840-1849)
T2  - ANNALES DE BRETAGNE ET DES PAYS DE L OUEST
LA  - French
KW  - BAY
AB  - In the early 1840s, the Bay of Brest flat oyster beds (Ostrea edulis) are depleted. This highly profitable activity, prized by fishermen, indirectly bolstered the number of registered sailors. The Navy, which had to be careful with this resource, commissioned an investigator to identify the causes of this impoverishment. Maerl dredging (Lithothamnium corallioides, Phymatholiton calcareum and Lithophyllum fasciculatum) is accused of damaging the oyster beds, and its exploitation was banned. At the same time, the Brest Agricultural Society was encouraging its use for a fertiliser usage and supply the "wasteland" of central Brittany. An arm wrestling opposes the Navy and the Brest Agricultural Society. Their influential and divergent interests prompted the Minister of the Navy to set up a joint commission in 1847, in charge of assessing the real repercussions of the amendment use. At the end of this investigation, the negotiations lead to a result deemed to be in the public interest. The Navy has finally adopted fisheries regulations, while the agricultural company is authorised to harvest maerl and red seaweed. But is this really favourable for all parties?
