AB  - Coastal ecosystems face increasing demands on their resources by tourism, fishing, energy construction, mining, residential development, and dredging. Documenting the impacts of these activities on benthic communities and substrates enables managers, planners, and monitors to gauge ecosystem health based on community assemblage, reef extent, and beach characteristics. Ecosystem engineers like Sabellaria vulgaris, oyster reefs, and coral reefs provide food, habitat, and biodiversity to areas typically devoid of such aspects. Due to the large spatial and temporal variance in worm reef ecosystems, traditional biologically sampling can be expensive and time-consuming. Combining biological sampling with aerial drone orthomosaics and digital elevation models enables accurate, rapid mapping of these unique shoreline stabilizing reefs when exposed during spring low tides. Reef structures were observed to trap sediment, attenuate wave energy, and increase subaerial beach volume following the 2016 winter storm season (December to April). Enhancement of biodiversity (abundance, biomass), sediment-trapping, and wave attenuation are performed by large aggregations of multigenerational worms only found within Delaware Bay, United States, thereby promoting the need for repeat monitoring and management to maintain these fragile benthic ecosystems.
