AB  - Maintaining environmental and societal roles of both coastal and inland waters necessitates developing operational models, products and services to provide short-term and long-term monitoring of their status. Remote sensing based approaches can complement conventional in situ methods efficiently by offering regular low-cost survey of large and remote areas. The Earth observation research programs of the Belgian Science Policy support four scientific and operational projects which focus on coastal and lake applications. The BEL-COLOUR project aims at improving the theoretical basis and software tools for products based on suspended matter, chlorophyll, yellow substances and dissolved organic matter in coastal and inland waters. The optical remote sensing based products have been developed in,I generic and exportable way and have been presented to potential users (fisheries, dredging operators, environmental managers, oceanographers). The ORMES project aims at developing, automating and implementing at the International Marine and Dredging Consultants (IMDC) a robust and user-friendly method to produce 2D sediment maps with ground measurements, hyperspectral airborne images and satellite images in estuarine zones. IMDC needs regular spatio-temporal information about the spatial repartition of turbidity in order to optimize the choice of dredging or dumping zones. The CORAL project focuses on the monitoring of coral reefs with hyperspectral remote sensing techniques in Eastern Indonesia. Indonesian coral reefs are threatened by human activities such as destructive or over-fishing practices and coastal development. The project aims at developing an efficient monitoring system of coral reefs based on remote sensing hyperspectral and multi-spectral imagery. The CLIMFISH project addresses the climate change impact on a sustainable use of Lake Tanganyika fisheries. In Lake Tanganyika, the abundance of fish species has changed dramatically in the last 30 years. If over-fishing is a possible cause of these changes in localized areas, natural variability and recent environmental changes must be taken into account. The objectives are to investigate the impact of recent environmental changes on the fisheries of Lake Tanganyika combining weather and limnological monitoring, fisheries statistics, remote sensing, data and eco-hydrodynamics modeling, and to evaluate the feasibility of developing an operational model as a forecasting tool for the managers of Lake Tanganyika fisheries.
