AB  - Camptoplax coppingeri Miers, 1884, has been known only from two specimens from Torres Strait, north Queensland, and its taxonomic affinities have been uncertain. A single new specimen has recently been dredged from the lagoon at Chesterfield Reefs, Coral Sea. It has typical pilumnid male abdomen and gonopods, and it is considered in overall facies to be rightfully placed in the Rhizopinae, most closely allied to Pseudolitochira Ward and Zehntneria Takeda. A lectotype has been designated from one of the two syntypes in the British Museum (Natural History).
