LA  - French
AB  - Sixty seawater and marine sediment samples were collected in the clear and dredged coral waters of a lagoon in Gaudeloupe. Total counts of 10-100 living bacteria were obtained by counting colonies on membranes after filtration and pour plate count techniques in the clear water coral zones. Living bacterial populations ranging from 2 .times. 103 to 20 .times. 103/ml of water were detected in the dredged coral zone. Suspended sandy particulate matter resulting from dredging seems to be the direct cause of the abnormal increase in bacteria. The mean values were .apprxeq. 103 living bacteria/ml for the coastal seawater and 104 for the brackish waters of channels in the mangrove swamp. It seems that the bacterial sediment population was under-estimated.
