TY  - JOUR
AU  - WILSON, JB
TI  - DISTRIBUTION OF THE CORAL LOPHELIA-PERTUSA (L) [L PROLIFERA (PALLAS)] IN THE NORTHEAST ATLANTIC
T2  - JOURNAL OF THE MARINE BIOLOGICAL ASSOCIATION OF THE UNITED KINGDOM
LA  - English
AB  - New data were compiled on the distribution of the deep-water coral L. pertusa (L.) derived from trawling records, grab and dredge stations and submersible dives, together with the few published records. The coral was widely distributed and occurred on all the offshore banks of the northeast Atlantic, with some records from the deepest water of the Minches and Sea of the Hebrides. It was comparatively rare on the upper continental slope and shelf edge to the west and north of Scotland [UK], and appeared less common there than previously suggested. It was fairly common in discrete patches on the flanks of Rockall Bank where it occurred at depths of 130-400 m (most records were from 220-350 m). The shallowest records were mostly from the southern part of the Bank. Detection of coral on echo-sounder records of the shelf edge, continental slope and Rockall Bank was difficult, particularly in areas of irregular topography, as colonies were generally only 1-1.5 m in height. Echo-sounder records from the deep water in the Sea of the Hebrides showed features up to 18 m in height thought to be related to coral banks. Under suitable conditions groups of colonies can be detected on sonographs (side-scan sonar records).
