TY  - JOUR
AU  - MOLINIER, R
TI  - ETUDE DES BIOCENOSES MARINES DU CAP CORSE
T2  - VEGETATIO
LA  - English
AB  - This northern tip of Corsica is described as to geology and climate. Using skin diving equipment, dredging, and sonic sounding the marine biocenoses surrounding the cape were studied by standard phytosociological methods. These methods are discussed and brought up to date. They apply without substantial change to studies of marine communities of plants and animals. The various schemes for classification of the vertical zones from high tide to the light limit for algal growth are compared and discussed. The supralittoral zone is occupied by a Verrucario-Melaraphetum neritoidis (8 stands tabulated) on rocky substrates and by others on mobile substrates. Their ecology and composition are discussed. The mesolittoral zone is occupied by a biocenose (12 stands tabulated) characterized by Chthamalus stellatus Zalokar of the order Chthamaletalia on rocks above and lower by a Neogoniolitho-Lithophylletum tortuosi (8 stands) or by a less frequent Nemodermetum tingitani (2 stands). Again mobile substrates (sand and shingle) have other biocenoses. Organogenic calcareous reef formations built up by Lithophyllum tortuosum (Esper) Foslie, are discussed as to petrology, chemistry, biology, and distribution around the cape. The platforms built by Vermetus cristatus Biondi are descriued in their various forms, their associated organisms listed, their distribution mapped, growth traced, and biocenoses they harbor in their various biotopes described. Mineralized pads formed by Corallina mediterranea Aresch. and Lithothamnium lenormandi (Aresch.) Foslie and projections of Lithophyllum incrustans Phil, are described with their associated animals and plants. Under the biocenoses of the infralittoral zone are described a photophilous, algal Cystoseiretum crinitae (30 stands) on rocky substrates, a photophilous Cystoseiretum strictae (5 stands) in wave-battered areas, and a sciaphilous Udoteo-Peyssonnelietum (2 stands), and in nitrogen-rich areas a Pterocladio-Ulvetum (6 stands) and others of more limited distribution. Stand surveys document these latter, and the species'' ecology and distribution are discussed. Phanerogamic associations include uncommon ones formed by Zostera nana Roth. and Cymodocea nodosa Asch., a more common biocenose forming a "sward", Posidonietum oceanicae (3 stands), and a sciaphilous biocenose on the rhizomes of the Posidonia, Udoteo-Peyssonnelietum (3 stands). The changes in balance between fauna and flora with increasing depth in these 2 biocenoses, their physiographic positions, and their ecology are discussed. In a still lower, sciaphilous zone data were obtained by dredging. Cross sections of the sea floor with indications of substrates and biocenoses are given and species lists for these biocenoses are discussed. The levels below possible algal growth are termed epibathyl, and their biocenoses are discussed. A map at 1 cm = 2.5 km shows the terrestrial plant communities of Cape Corse and the surrounding marine biocenoses. These communities are discussed and certain parallels between the terrestrial arrangement of communities and the marine are pointed out. With distance from the line of the sea both become less fragmentary and less specialized until climax associations (Quercetum ilicis and Posidonietum) are met. Further distance again leads to more specialized ecological conditions and impoverishment of the communities. 126 references.
