TY  - JOUR
AU  - Stephenson, LW
TI  - Geology and paleontology of the Georges Bank canyons Part II Upper Cretaceous fossils from Georges Bank (including species from Banquereau, Nova Scotia)
T2  - BULLETIN OF THE GEOLOGICAL SOCIETY OF AMERICA
LA  - English
AB  - Describes and illustrates Up. Cretaceous fossils dredged from the Atlantic Ocean at 2 localities, one off the coast of New England, the other off the coast of Nova Scotia; one from conglomeratic sandstone in place on the side of Gorge I, one of the submerged gorges cutting the edge of the continental shelf along the south side of Georges Bank (depth between 480 and 596 meters), at latitude N. 40[degree]24[image]30[image], longitude W. 69[degree]07[image]30[image]; the other from a loose boulder of shell marl (depth 200 fathoms), pre-sumabry dislodged from its parent stratum by the shove of glacial ice during Pleistocene time, on the eastern part of Banquereau, Nova Scotia. At the Georges Bank locality the rock yielded 2 species of Serpula (not specifically named), 1 echinoid, 1 bryozoan, described by R. S. Bassler [see B. A. 11(1) entry 2156], 24 pelecy-pods (3 new; 12 not specifically named), and 7 or 8 gastropods (1 new; the others not specifically named). The fauna indicates an Up. Cretaceous age belonging to either the upper part of the Exogyra ponderosa zone or the lower part of the Exogyra costata zone (upper Campanian or lower Maestrichtian). The new spp. are: (Echinodermata) Hardouinia (?) stetsoni* (p.371); (Mollusca) Postligata schalki* (p.374); Cardium (Crio-cardium) atlanticanum* (p.378); Donax (?) atlantis* (p.369); and Cerithium georgesensis* (p.384). The Banquereau fauna includes 1 coral, Parasmilia (?) sp., 1 unnamed species of Serpula, 19 pelecypods (12 new; 7 not specifically named), 2 species of Dcntalium (not specifically named), and 7 or more gastropods (5 new; the others not specifically named). This is an early Up. Cretaceous fauna new to the Atlantic Coast of N. Amcr.; the fauna suggests an age relationship with a largely undescribed fauna from the Woodbine formation of Texas (Cenomanian) and may be a shallow marine facies corresponding in age to the Raritan formation of New Jersey. The new species are: (Mollusca) Acila banquereauensis* (p.387); Nuculana piscatoria* (p.38S); Brcviarca scotiensis* (p.388); Pleria (?) dalli* (p.389); Pcctcn (Camptoncctes) microcancelli* (p.391); Lucina olsoni* (p.392); Isocardia novensis* (p.393); Aphrodina marina* (p.393); Cyclorisma elongata* (p.395); Leguinea intermedium (p.396); Corbula latona* (p.397); C. tethys* (p.398); Turritella macneili* (p.399); T. thomsonina* (p.400); Anchura potana* (p.401); Voluto-derma (?) venusta* (p.402); and Avellana pelagana* (p.403). The faunas described prove the existence of a submerged northward extension of the Atlantic Coastal Plain off the coasts of New England and Nova Scotia.
